---

<ProjectLayout
	projectName="SCoDe"
	navItems={[
		{ label: "Abstract", href: "#abstract-heading" },
		{ label: "Method", href: "#method-heading" },
		{ label: "Evaluation", href: "#evaluation-heading" },
		{ label: "Results", href: "#results-heading" },
		{ label: "Citation", href: "#citation-heading" },
		{ label: "References", href: "#references-heading" },
	]}
	meta={{
		title: "SCoDe",
		description: "Scale-aware Co-visible Region Detection for Image Matching.",
	}}
>
	<article class="project-page">
		<ProjectHero
			year="2025"
			title="Scale-aware Co-visible Region Detection for Image Matching"
			{authors}
			{affiliations}
			notes={[{ mark: "*", text: "Corresponding author" }]}
			venue={{
				label: "ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing",
				href: "https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/isprs-journal-of-photogrammetry-and-remote-sensing",
			}}
			{links}
		/>

		<section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="abstract-heading">
			<div class="section-label"><span>01</span><h2 id="abstract-heading">Abstract</h2></div>
			<div class="abstract-layout">
				<p class="project-abstract">
					Large scale differences weaken appearance consistency and make keypoint localization
					uncertain. SCoDe<sup><a href="#ref-1">[1]</a></sup>—Scale-aware Co-visible Region
					Detector—introduces an intermediate region-level correspondence before point matching: it
					detects the content jointly visible in both images, aligns those regions in scale, and
					then confines fine correspondence estimation to them. Scale Head Attention correlates
					features across multiple scale subspaces, while learnable queries aggregate evidence from
					both views. This coarse-to-fine formulation improves matching precision by up to 8.41% and
					remains effective across different matching backbones.
				</p>
				<figure class="abstract-figure">
					<div class="figure-media">
						{corners.map((p) => <span class={`figure-cross figure-cross--${p}`} aria-hidden="true">+</span>)}<Image
							src={intro}
							alt="Direct point matching and hierarchical SCoDe matching"
							loading="eager"
							width={1200}
						/>
					</div><figcaption>
						<strong>Fig. 1.</strong> Direct point matching remains ambiguous under drastic scale variation.
						SCoDe first detects and rescales co-visible regions, then establishes fine correspondences
						within aligned support.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
			<div class="section-label"><span>02</span><h2 id="method-heading">Method</h2></div>
			<figure class="pipeline-figure">
				<div class="figure-media">
					{corners.map((p) => <span class={`figure-cross figure-cross--${p}`} aria-hidden="true">+</span>)}<Image
						src={pipeline}
						alt="SCoDe architecture"
						loading="lazy"
					/>
				</div><figcaption>
					<strong>Fig. 2.</strong>
					<strong>SCoDe architecture.</strong> A shared CNN encoder produces paired features; Scale Head
					Attention exchanges information across scale subspaces; learnable queries decode anchor points,
					border offsets, and confidence for the co-visible regions.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<figure class="sha-figure">
				<div class="figure-media">
					{corners.map((p) => <span class={`figure-cross figure-cross--${p}`} aria-hidden="true">+</span>)}<Image
						src={sha}
						alt="Scale Head Attention"
						loading="lazy"
					/>
				</div><figcaption>
					<strong>Fig. 3.</strong> Scale Head Cross-Attention alternates the query view and projects features
					through convolution groups with different receptive-field scales.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<div class="method-pair">
				<figure>
					<div class="figure-media equal-method">
						{corners.map((p) => <span class={`figure-cross figure-cross--${p}`} aria-hidden="true">+</span>)}<Image
							src={decoder}
							alt="Learnable query decoder"
							loading="lazy"
						/>
					</div><figcaption>
						<strong>Fig. 4.</strong> The decoder combines self-correlation among learnable queries with
						cross-attention to scale-aware paired features.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure>
					<div class="figure-media equal-method">
						{corners.map((p) => <span class={`figure-cross figure-cross--${p}`} aria-hidden="true">+</span>)}<Image
							src={confidence}
							alt="Co-visible confidence head"
							loading="lazy"
						/>
					</div><figcaption>
						<strong>Fig. 5.</strong> Co-visible confidence combines pseudo-cosine similarity with a learnable
						binary regression branch, allowing the model to reject image pairs without valid shared content.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
			</div>
			<div class="method-grid">
				<article>
					<span>01</span><h3>Hierarchical matching</h3><p>
						Region alignment reduces scale and semantic ambiguity before local descriptors establish
						point correspondences.
					</p>
				</article><article>
					<span>02</span><h3>Scale Head Attention</h3><p>
						Multiple scale-specific subspaces model correlations that conventional same-scale
						attention can miss.
					</p>
				</article><article>
					<span>03</span><h3>Validity-aware prediction</h3><p>
						Anchors, border offsets, and confidence jointly describe where co-visible support exists
						and whether a valid region should be returned.
					</p>
				</article>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="evaluation-heading">
			<div class="section-label"><span>03</span><h2 id="evaluation-heading">Evaluation</h2></div>
			<p class="project-abstract">
				Training and primary evaluation use MegaDepth<sup><a href="#ref-2">[2]</a></sup>, with 3,000
				test pairs selected from ten scenes and ray-cast co-visible ground truth. Region detection
				is measured with confidence recall, IoU recall, overlap IoU (OIoU), and mean IoU. Downstream
				matching uses pose AUC, accuracy, mAA, precision, and matching score across classical,
				sparse learned, SuperGlue<sup><a href="#ref-4">[4]</a></sup>, and dense LoFTR<sup
					><a href="#ref-5">[5]</a></sup
				> pipelines. We compare region guidance against OETR<sup><a href="#ref-3">[3]</a></sup>.
				ScanNet<sup><a href="#ref-6">[6]</a></sup> and GL3D<sup><a href="#ref-7">[7]</a></sup> provide
				qualitative cross-domain tests.
			</p>
			<div class="evaluation-grid">
				<div><strong>81.98%</strong><span>mIoU for co-visible region detection</span></div><div>
					<strong>99%+</strong><span>confidence recall at all reported thresholds</span>
				</div><div>
					<strong>+8.41%</strong><span>maximum downstream matching precision gain</span>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="evaluation-tables">
				<figure class="paper-table-figure" data-sync-table-caption>
					<div
						class="table-scroll"
						tabindex="0"
						role="region"
						aria-label="MegaDepth co-visible region detection results"
					>
						<table class="paper-table">
							<thead
								><tr><th scope="col">Method</th><th scope="col">IoU@.5</th><th scope="col">IoU@.75</th><th scope="col">OIoU@.9</th><th scope="col">mIoU</th></tr
								></thead
							><tbody
								><tr><th scope="row">OETR*</th><td>87.40</td><td>60.71</td><td>70.90</td><td>76.71</td></tr><tr
									><th scope="row">OETR (cyclecenter)</th><td>90.71</td><td>59.03</td><td>52.36</td><td
										>77.29</td
									></tr
								><tr class="ours"
									><th scope="row">SCoDe</th><td><strong>94.69</strong></td><td><strong>71.43</strong></td><td
										><strong>74.82</strong></td
									><td><strong>81.98</strong></td></tr
								></tbody
							>
						</table>
					</div><figcaption class="table-caption">
						<strong>Table 1.</strong> Co-visible region detection on MegaDepth (%). OETR* is retrained
						under the same conditions.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure class="paper-table-figure" data-sync-table-caption>
					<div
						class="table-scroll"
						tabindex="0"
						role="region"
						aria-label="MegaDepth ablation results"
					>
						<table class="paper-table">
							<thead
								><tr><th scope="col">Variant</th><th scope="col">mIoU</th><th scope="col">Conf</th><th scope="col">IoU</th><th scope="col">OIoU</th></tr></thead
							><tbody
								><tr
									><th scope="row">w/o ScaleHead</th><td>77.29</td><td>98.69</td><td>62.42</td><td>88.61</td
									></tr
								><tr><th scope="row">w/o DWConv</th><td>78.41</td><td>98.74</td><td>64.03</td><td>87.19</td></tr
								><tr
									><th scope="row">w/o confidence</th><td>79.86</td><td>–</td><td>66.53</td><td
										><strong>89.18</strong></td
									></tr
								><tr class="ours"
									><th scope="row">SCoDe</th><td><strong>82.02</strong></td><td><strong>99.62</strong></td><td
										><strong>69.82</strong></td
									><td>87.66</td></tr
								></tbody
							>
						</table>
					</div><figcaption class="table-caption">
						<strong>Table 2.</strong> MegaDepth ablation (%). Recall columns report the mean across their
						respective thresholds.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="results-heading">
			<div class="section-label"><span>04</span><h2 id="results-heading">Results</h2></div>
			<p class="results-intro">
				SCoDe’s gains grow as scale ratios become more severe: accurate region boundaries remove
				unrelated content, aligned crops reduce repetitive-pattern ambiguity, and the same prior
				improves otherwise very different local matching architectures.
			</p>
			<figure class="main-results-table" data-sync-table-caption>
				<div
					class="table-scroll"
					tabindex="0"
					role="region"
					aria-label="MegaDepth large-scale image matching results"
				>
					<table class="paper-table">
						<thead
							><tr
								><th scope="col">Pipeline</th><th scope="col">Prior</th><th scope="col">AUC@5</th><th scope="col">AUC@10</th><th scope="col">AUC@20</th><th
									scope="col">Acc@5</th
								><th scope="col">Acc@10</th><th scope="col">Acc@15</th><th scope="col">Acc@20</th><th scope="col">mAA@5</th><th scope="col">mAA@10</th><th
									scope="col">mAA@20</th
								><th scope="col">P</th><th scope="col">MS</th></tr
							></thead
						>
						<tbody
							>{
								matchingRows.map((row) => (
									<tr class={row.prior === "+SCoDe" ? "ours" : undefined}>
										<th scope="row">{row.pipeline}</th>
										<td>{row.prior}</td>
										{row.values.map((value) => (
											<td>{row.prior === "+SCoDe" ? <strong>{value}</strong> : value}</td>
										))}
									</tr>
								))
							}</tbody
						>
					</table>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="table-caption">
					<strong>Table 3.</strong> Complete MegaDepth evaluation for image pairs with scale ratio greater
					than 2 (%). Each feature–matcher pipeline is evaluated without a region prior, with OETR, and
					with SCoDe. SCoDe consistently improves pose estimation, matching precision, and matching score
					across sparse, graph-based, and dense paradigms.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<div class="results-grid">
				<figure class="wide">
					<div class="figure-media">
						{corners.map((p) => <span class={`figure-cross figure-cross--${p}`} aria-hidden="true">+</span>)}<Image
							src={examples}
							alt="Qualitative MegaDepth comparisons"
							loading="lazy"
							width={1400}
						/>
					</div><figcaption>
						<strong>Fig. 6.</strong> MegaDepth qualitative comparison. SCoDe remains accurate when OETR
						misses small shared regions, shifts the detected area, or returns overly broad support.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure>
					<div class="figure-media square-media">
						{corners.map((p) => <span class={`figure-cross figure-cross--${p}`} aria-hidden="true">+</span>)}<Image
							src={radarLoftr}
							alt="LoFTR resolution-aware gains"
							loading="lazy"
							width={1200}
						/>
					</div><figcaption>
						<strong>Fig. 7.</strong> Relative gains for LoFTR at 480² and 640² input resolutions.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure>
					<div class="figure-media square-media">
						{corners.map((p) => <span class={`figure-cross figure-cross--${p}`} aria-hidden="true">+</span>)}<Image
							src={radarSs}
							alt="SuperPoint SuperGlue resolution-aware gains"
							loading="lazy"
							width={1200}
						/>
					</div><figcaption>
						<strong>Fig. 8.</strong> Relative gains for SuperPoint + SuperGlue. SCoDe remains stable when
						the input resolution changes.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure class="wide">
					<div class="figure-media">
						{corners.map((p) => <span class={`figure-cross figure-cross--${p}`} aria-hidden="true">+</span>)}<Image
							src={rotation}
							alt="Rotation robustness"
							loading="lazy"
							width={1800}
						/>
					</div><figcaption>
						<strong>Fig. 9.</strong> Correct-match ratio under 0°–50° in-plane rotation. Region constraints
						improve DISK, R2D2, D2-Net, and SuperPoint without explicit orientation modeling.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure class="wide">
					<div class="figure-media domain-pair">
						{corners.map((p) => <span class={`figure-cross figure-cross--${p}`} aria-hidden="true">+</span>)}<Image
							src={scannet}
							alt="ScanNet generalization"
							loading="lazy"
							width={1400}
						/><Image
							src={gl3d}
							alt="GL3D generalization"
							loading="lazy"
							width={1400}
						/>
					</div><figcaption>
						<strong>Fig. 10.</strong> Generalization to ScanNet indoor scenes (top) and GL3D outdoor/aerial
						scenes (bottom). Blue boxes are accurate regions; red boxes mark failure cases under extreme
						scale, viewpoint, or rotation changes.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="citation-heading">
			<div class="section-label"><span>05</span><h2 id="citation-heading">Citation</h2></div><div
				class="citation"
			>
				<pre><code>{bibtex}</code></pre><button data-copy-bibtex>Copy BibTeX</button>
			</div>
		</section>
		<section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="references-heading">
			<div class="section-label"><span>06</span><h2 id="references-heading">References</h2></div><ol
				class="references-list"
			>
				<li id="ref-1">
					X. Pan, Z. Xia, and X. Zheng. “Scale-aware Co-visible Region Detection for Image
					Matching.” ISPRS JPRS, 2025. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2025.08.015"
						>DOI</a
					>
				</li><li id="ref-2">
					Z. Li and N. Snavely. “MegaDepth.” CVPR, 2018. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.00607"
						>arXiv</a
